class GenericRelation {
  definition {
    ISO 704 / ISO 1087-1 / ISO 12620 generic relation.

    Connects a comprehensive concept (the genus) to two or more
    specific concepts (the species) which together constitute a
    decomposition by some criterion of subdivision.

    Shown as a rake or bracket in source diagrams (ISO 704 §5.5.4.1).
    All species within one relation are coordinate concepts (ISO 12620):
    they share the comprehensive AND share the criterion.

    Distinct from binary RelatedConcept edges broader_generic /
    narrower_generic, which express pairwise genus–species assertions
    without completeness or presence/count claims. A single species
    should be expressed as a binary edge, not a GenericRelation.

    Per-file storage: lives at
    `relations/<comprehensive-id>/<criterion-slug>.yaml`. See
    docs/design/relations-as-files.md.

    Mirrors AbstractNaryRelation shape. Field `comprehensive` denotes
    the genus concept for GenericRelation.

    Justified by OIML V 2-200:2010 evidence — see
    docs/design/generic-relation.md.
  }
  +comprehensive: <<StandardDocument>> ReferenceToTermbase [1] {
    definition {
      The genus concept — the superordinate in a genus–species rake.
      Encoded as a ConceptRef (source + id, optional text).

      May reference an ExternalConcept when the genus is a parenthetical
      term defined elsewhere (e.g., "(precision condition of measurement)"
      in OIML V 2-200:2010).
    }
  }
  +members: GenericMember [2..*] {
    definition {
      The species concepts. Each member carries a ConceptRef plus
      ISO 704:2022 presence, count, and delimiting metadata.

      ISO 704 requires "two or more." A single species should be
      expressed as a binary broader_generic edge instead.
    }
  }
  +completeness: Completeness [0..1] {
    definition {
      Whether the encoded species fitted together constitute the
      comprehensive (complete) or only some of it (partial — other
      species exist that are not encoded).

      Defaults to complete when omitted.
    }
  }
  +criterion: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      The principle by which the genus is divided into species.
      ISO 12620 coordinate-concept coherence: species within one
      relation share the comprehensive AND the criterion.

      Two GenericRelations on the same comprehensive with the same
      criterion are duplicates (validation error). Two with different
      criteria are distinct decompositions.

      OIML V 2-200:2010 example: 5.1 measurement standard has 6
      GenericRelations, distinguished by criteria such as
      "by realization medium", "by governance level", etc.

      Multilingual (relation-level metadata).
    }
  }
  +sources: ConceptSource [0..*] {
    definition {
      Bibliographic references for this specific generic relation.
    }
  }
  +notes: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      Free-form localized commentary on this relation.
    }
  }
  +status: ConceptStatus [0..1] {
    definition {
      Lifecycle status of this relation, independent of the
      comprehensive concept's status.
    }
  }
}
